<speak>
<p>A Gaussian integer is a plus b i, where a and b are integers. Modulo one plus i, two numbers agree when their difference is a Gaussian integer multiple of one plus i. To test divisibility, multiply by one minus i over one minus i. The denominator becomes two. Collect real and imaginary parts: the quantity a plus b divided by two, plus i times the quantity b minus a divided by two.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Both halves are integers exactly when a and b have the same parity. Two even integers work, and two odd integers work. Different parities give two noninteger halves. Thus one plus i divides a plus b i exactly when a plus b is even.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>There are only two residues. Send a plus b i to a plus b modulo two. For three plus i, the sum is four, giving residue zero. For three plus two i, the sum is five, giving residue one. The output records arithmetic parity, not a complex angle.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>This map preserves addition. It preserves multiplication too: the real part of a product is a c minus b d, and the imaginary part is a d plus b c. Modulo two, minus and plus agree, so their sum is the product of a plus b and c plus d. The map is onto the two-element field, and its kernel consists exactly of multiples of one plus i.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>In particular, one, negative one, i, and negative i all have residue one. The factor identities explain the key congruences: i minus one equals i times the quantity one plus i, and two equals the product of one plus i and one minus i. Both signs become one.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>For any positive integer u, apply the map to each polynomial coefficient. The powers of P and the relation P to the u squared equals one remain in place. Only the coefficients change. For example, i minus P reduces to one plus P. This equality also respects the cycle relation, even when u is one.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Keep the order of operations clear. In defining E of P, first form the exact integral quotients supplied by the common signs. Only then reduce their coefficients. Both two and one plus i become zero in the quotient, so neither can be used as a denominator there. We carry forward two facts: multiples of i minus one disappear, and both signs have residue one.<break time="1200ms"/></p>
</speak>
